module co-district-court-civil.civil

// Civil actions in the Colorado district courts, from the defending side's
// view of outcomes. Every period below is stated in a note, not counted:
// computation is C.R.C.P. 6(a), with Colorado's legal holidays, for which
// lit/core has no counting rule. The filing and jury fees are set by
// statute, which the vendored rules do not state. The outline's one named
// decision, Warne v. Hall, 2016 CO 50, 373 P.3d 588, is kept as the
// compilation's annotation to C.R.C.P. 12 reports it.
start commenced

state commenced: "Action commenced" by applicant cite "C.R.C.P. 3"
{
  note "A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the court or by service of a summons and complaint; if commenced by service, the complaint must be filed within 14 days after service, or the service of summons is deemed ineffective and void (C.R.C.P. 3(a)). If a defendant is not served within 63 days (nine weeks) after the complaint is filed, the court, on motion or on its own after notice to the plaintiff, dismisses the action without prejudice against that defendant or orders service within a specified time; on good cause shown it extends the time for service (C.R.C.P. 4(m))."
  edge-commenced-served "Defendant served" -> answer-due {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(08), C.R.C.P. 12(a)"
  }
  edge-commenced-unserved "Not served within 63 days" -> dismissed-unserved {
    authority cite "C.R.C.P. 4(m)"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-unserved: "Dismissed without prejudice for want of service" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "C.R.C.P. 4(m)"

decision answer-due: "Answer, motion or default" by office cite "Rule Change 2024(08), C.R.C.P. 12(a)"
{
  note "A defendant files an answer or other response within 21 days after service of the summons and complaint, except as otherwise provided by rule or statute; if the court denies a Rule 12 motion or postpones it to trial, the responsive pleading is filed within 14 days after notice of the court's action, and if it grants a motion for a more definite statement, within 14 days after service of that statement (Rule Change 2024(08), C.R.C.P. 12(a)). If the complaint is not served with the summons, or the summons is served outside Colorado or by publication, the time is 35 days after service (C.R.C.P. 12(a))."
  edge-answer-due-default "No response" -> default-entered { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 55(a)" }
  edge-answer-due-motion "Rule 12(b) motion filed" -> pleading-motion {
    authority cite "C.R.C.P. 12(b)"
  }
  edge-answer-due-answer "Answer filed" -> discovery {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(08), C.R.C.P. 12(a)"
  }
}

decision pleading-motion: "Motion to dismiss decided" by court cite "C.R.C.P. 12(b)"
{
  note "The defenses that may be made by separate motion filed on or before the date the answer is due include failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted (C.R.C.P. 12(b)). The compilation's annotation to the rule reports that Warne v. Hall, 2016 CO 50, 373 P.3d 588, adopted for a section (b)(5) motion the plausible-on-its-face standard of the federal courts (C.R.C.P. 12). Unless the court orders otherwise, the responding party has 21 days after the filing of a motion to file a responsive brief (14 days if the motion is filed 42 days or less before trial), the moving party 7 days after the responsive brief to reply, and a motion and response not under C.R.C.P. 12(b)(1) or (2) or 56 are limited to 15 pages and a reply to 10 (Rule Change 2024(03), C.R.C.P. 121)."
  edge-pleading-motion-dismissed "Granted" -> dismissed-pleading { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 12(b)" }
  edge-pleading-motion-denied "Denied: responsive pleading within 14 days after notice" -> discovery {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(08), C.R.C.P. 12(a)"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-pleading: "Dismissed on the motion" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "C.R.C.P. 12(b)"

terminal default-entered: "Default entered" outcome [loss] cite "C.R.C.P. 55(a)"
{
  note "When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought has failed to plead or otherwise defend and that is made to appear by affidavit or otherwise, the clerk enters the default (C.R.C.P. 55(a)). A party entitled to a default judgment applies to the court; a party who has appeared is served with written notice of the application at least 7 days before the hearing (C.R.C.P. 55(b)). For good cause shown the court may set aside an entry of default, and a default judgment in accordance with Rule 60(b) (C.R.C.P. 55(c)). Outcome from the defending side."
}

state discovery: "Pleadings closed; discovery" by applicant cite "C.R.C.P. 26"
{
  note "Discovery is governed by C.R.C.P. 26 through 37. A party demands a jury by filing and serving a demand not later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue and paying the jury fee; another party may demand a jury on other issues within 14 days after service of a demand limited to some issues; failure to file and serve a demand and pay the fee waives trial by jury (C.R.C.P. 38(b)-(e))."
  edge-discovery-sj "Summary judgment motion filed" -> sj-motion { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 56(c)" }
  edge-discovery-trial "No dispositive motion" -> trial { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 38(b)" }
  edge-discovery-settle "Settled or voluntarily dismissed" -> settled {
    authority cite "C.R.C.P. 41(a)"
  }
}

decision sj-motion: "Summary judgment decided" by court cite "C.R.C.P. 56(c)"
{
  note "A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement or after the adverse party moves; a defending party may move at any time (C.R.C.P. 56(a), (b)). Unless the court orders otherwise, a motion for summary judgment is filed no later than 91 days (13 weeks) before trial and a cross-motion no later than 70 days (10 weeks) before trial; judgment is rendered forthwith if there is no genuine issue as to any material fact and the moving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (C.R.C.P. 56(c)). The response is due 21 days after the filing of the motion and the reply 14 days after the response, and a motion and response under C.R.C.P. 56 are limited to 25 pages and a reply to 15 (Rule Change 2024(03), C.R.C.P. 121)."
  edge-sj-motion-granted "Granted" -> judgment-for-movant { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 56(c)" }
  edge-sj-motion-denied "Denied or partly denied" -> trial { authority cite "C.R.C.P. 56(d)" }
}

terminal judgment-for-movant: "Summary judgment for the movant" outcome [win, judgment] cite "C.R.C.P. 56(c)"
{
  note "Summary judgment on liability alone and an order specifying the facts without substantial controversy (C.R.C.P. 56(c), (d)) are not modeled. Appeal from the judgment is not modeled on this path."
}

state trial: "Trial" by court cite "C.R.C.P. 50"
{
  note "A party may move for a directed verdict at the close of the evidence offered by an opponent or at the close of all the evidence, stating the specific grounds (C.R.C.P. 50)."
  edge-trial-post "Verdict or decision; judgment entered" -> post-trial {
    authority cite "C.R.C.P. 58(a)"
  }
}

decision post-trial: "Post-trial motions decided" by court cite "C.R.C.P. 59(a)"
{
  note "Within 14 days of entry of judgment, or such greater time as the court allows on a request made within that period, a party may move for a new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, amendment of findings or amendment of judgment (C.R.C.P. 59(a)). On a motion supported by affidavit the opposing party has 21 days after service of the affidavit to file opposing affidavits (C.R.C.P. 59(d)). The court determines a post-trial motion within 63 days (9 weeks) of its filing; one not decided by then is deemed denied for all purposes, including C.A.R. 4(a) (C.R.C.P. 59(j))."
  edge-post-trial-judgment "Motions resolved or none filed" -> judgment-entered {
    authority cite "C.R.C.P. 58(a)"
  }
}

state judgment-entered: "Final judgment entered" by office cite "C.R.C.P. 58(a)"
{
  note "The effective date of entry of judgment is the actual date of the signing of the written judgment; entry is not delayed for the taxing of costs (C.R.C.P. 58(a)). A motion for relief for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect or fraud is made within a reasonable time and not more than 182 days after the judgment (C.R.C.P. 60(b))."
  edge-judgment-entered-appeal "Next step" -> appeal-decision { authority cite "C.A.R. 4(a)" }
}

decision appeal-decision: "Notice of appeal within 49 days of entry?" by applicant cite "C.A.R. 4(a)"
{
  note "The notice of appeal is filed with the appellate court, with an advisory copy served on the lower court, within 49 days after entry of the judgment; once a timely notice is filed any other party may file a notice within 14 days after it. A timely C.R.C.P. 59 motion terminates the running of the time for all parties, which runs from the order disposing of the last such motion or its deemed denial under C.R.C.P. 59(j). On a showing of excusable neglect the appellate court may extend the time by not more than 35 days. If notice of entry is transmitted by mail or E-Service, the time runs from the mailing or E-Service (C.A.R. 4(a))."
  edge-appeal-decision-taken "Notice filed" -> appeal-taken { authority cite "C.A.R. 3(a)" }
  edge-appeal-decision-none "No notice filed" -> final-no-appeal { authority cite "C.A.R. 4(a)" }
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken: continue in the Colorado appellate package" outcome [appeal] cite "C.A.R. 3(a)"
{
  note "Hand-off to lit/co-state-appellate (co-appellate-civil)."
}

terminal final-no-appeal: "Final: no appeal" outcome [final] cite "C.A.R. 4(a)"

terminal settled: "Settled or voluntarily dismissed" outcome [settlement] cite "C.R.C.P. 41(a)"
{
  note "A plaintiff may dismiss without order of court on payment of costs by notice before the adverse party files or serves an answer or a motion for summary judgment, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; unless stated otherwise the dismissal is without prejudice (C.R.C.P. 41(a)(1))."
}
